Beyond Plausibility
There is more to the law of pleading—and the practice of pleading—than Rule 8(a)(2) and plausibility. We now move beyond plausibility pleading to explore many other factors, both legal and practical, that influence or constrain the ways that lawyers draft complaints.

We will explore the use of pleading in the alternative, a longstanding practice that allows lawyers to allege facts in the alternative, essentially offering two different versions of the story of what happened, while acknowledging that only one of them can be true. As you read about pleading in the alternative, ask yourself: When might it be the best option for a plaintiff? When might it be the only option? And why might pleading in alternative be a bad idea?

We will study Rule 11, which empowers the court to impose sanctions on parties or attorneys who submit pleadings to the court for improper purposes or without sufficient grounding in research on the law and investigation of the facts. The allegations in a complaint need not be based on irrefutable evidence, but a lawyer can’t simply make them up. As you read about Rule 11, ask yourself: Other than Rule 11, what other factors might keep a lawyer from filing pleadings based on flimsy legal arguments and “creative” factual allegations? Is Rule 11 the only line of defense against frivolous claims? Is it an important line of defense? Is it redundant with other constraints on a lawyer’s conduct?

We will study heightened pleading standards, which are special, stricter pleading standards that Rules or statutes impose on specific types of allegations or legal claims. Comparing these elevated pleading standards to plausibility pleading helps calibrate exactly how strict we should expect plausibility pleading to be in practice. And by looking beyond the United States to the pleading rules of other countries, we can make an even broader, and possibly more illuminating, set of comparisons.

But first, set aside rules and doctrine for a moment, and ask: Even if there were no pleading standards at all—no complaint could ever be dismissed for failure to state a claim—what would complaints look like? Why might lawyers write detailed—or at least “plausible”—complaints regardless?


